#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebec626a-be3d-5920-9588-48f3df108944" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Two-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30cbc511-46f2-57a7-a1e6-5b9df41e93f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362597} {:answer/id #uuid "67e259c2-66b9-50e9-b578-1c42714caf20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362598} {:answer/id #uuid "c6bb899f-cddb-5bbd-8b15-deb369bf541e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362599} {:answer/id #uuid "e33d1235-04f0-5510-b143-ff55309b0743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{7}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362600} {:answer/id #uuid "32a85622-d88c-5334-b282-e0da8268939c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\sqrt{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30cbc511-46f2-57a7-a1e6-5b9df41e93f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362597} :answer-field/id #uuid "b819c7c7-4de5-5d16-a83f-aab407f3ff76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "24eb3f60-8596-5c16-bdd4-fd742e474035" :question/math-academy-id "q-106084" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)=2{x}^{3}-y$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨2,2⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{3}-y$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨2,2⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,2)-f(0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,2⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\ (2(2)^{3}-2)-(2(0)^{3}-1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 15 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨6{x}^{2},-1⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨6{c}_{1}^{2},-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 15 & =⟨6{c}_{1}^{2},-1⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 15 & =12{c}_{1}^{2}-1 \\\\ 16 & =12{c}_{1}^{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}<0<2,$ it's clear that $-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$ corresponds to the $x$-coordinate of a point that does not lie on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Therefore, we can ignore it. Thus, ${c}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}$ to find ${c}_{2}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{2-1} & =\\frac{x-0}{2-0} \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking into account that ${c}_{1}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},$ we obtain that ${c}_{2}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}+1=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}+1.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}},\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}+1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124607 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f9ea905-6eb5-55fe-b487-b115406bbc46" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Three-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "827f14ed-373b-54f0-87eb-ba05ac897597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362604} {:answer/id #uuid "344eb24e-1156-549a-aa63-f7b70c503bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362605} {:answer/id #uuid "9fa8cd0a-d89b-5bf5-ac6c-3d535195e448" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{5}{6},\\frac{1}{3},-\\frac{2}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362606} {:answer/id #uuid "be65387c-8a7a-5830-b531-fb3b8cd8edc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362607} {:answer/id #uuid "532d3b94-1153-561c-80f6-cf5aaf59db52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3},-\\frac{2}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "827f14ed-373b-54f0-87eb-ba05ac897597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362604} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec63c256-7f17-50ef-ab3a-8b079a43b2f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cccbf905-9f63-54b3-9f8d-d6f0dbce85ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-106251" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,2,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,0,-1⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,2,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,0,-1⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0,-1)-f(0,2,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,0,-1⟩-⟨0,2,1⟩) \\\\ 2-(-7) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-2,-2⟩ \\\\ 9 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x,-4y,2z⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1},-4{c}_{2},2{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-2,-2⟩ \\\\ 9 & =⟨2{c}_{1},-4{c}_{2},2{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-2,-2⟩ \\\\ 9 & =2{c}_{1}+8{c}_{2}-4{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-2}{(-2)}=\\frac{z-1}{(-2)} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{y-2}{2}=-\\frac{z-1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,\\,y=2-2t,\\,z=1-2t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t,\\,\\,{c}_{2}=2-2t,\\,{c}_{3}=1-2t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $2{c}_{1}+8{c}_{2}-4{c}_{3}=9,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(t)+8(2-2t)-4(1-2t) & =9 \\\\ 2t+16-16t-4+8t & =9 \\\\ -6t & =-3 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2-2(\\frac{1}{2})=1, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-2(\\frac{1}{2})=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(\\frac{1}{2},1,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399517 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124607 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f9ea905-6eb5-55fe-b487-b115406bbc46" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Three-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b63ab9ff-56cc-569e-a2eb-37c7b01b50d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399519} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb712d9-a5c5-5d8c-831a-ab2ad2eb58be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{2})$ and $(0,1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399520} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e7b9b8-1b51-5d9a-a752-82b801722063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$ and $(0,1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399521} {:answer/id #uuid "39a8fbcf-5380-5a95-af67-4ee1a6d74327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0,1)$ and $(-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399522} {:answer/id #uuid "55cd1cb9-8166-5d71-80af-8f3412ded7b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0,1)$ and $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{2})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0e7b9b8-1b51-5d9a-a752-82b801722063" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3})$ and $(0,1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399521} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7e2b45d-5b9d-5091-9941-06bd82a064b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6a0cb1f4-667e-57bd-8d19-2b32f34f6a8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-106585" :question/problem "Find the points that satisfy the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-{z}^{3}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨-1,0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨0,1,0⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-{z}^{3}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨-1,0,1⟩$ and $b=⟨0,1,0⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1,0)-f(-1,0,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨0,1,0⟩-⟨-1,0,1⟩) \\\\ 0-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x,0,-3{z}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1},0,-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =⟨2{c}_{1},0,-3{c}_{3}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ 0 & =2{c}_{1}+3{c}_{3}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-(-1)}{1} & =\\frac{y-0}{1}=\\frac{z-1}{(-1)} \\\\ x+1 & =y=1-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t-1\\,\\,y=t,\\,z=1-t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t-1\\,\\,{c}_{2}=t,\\,{c}_{3}=1-t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $2{c}_{1}+3{c}_{3}^{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(t-1)+3(1-t)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (t-1)[2+3(t-1)] & =0 \\\\ (t-1)(3t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{3},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we proceed to find the coordinates of the points that satisfy the mean value theorem.\n\n- For $t=\\frac{1}{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}-1=-\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the first point $C$ are $(-\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{2}{3}).$\n\n- For $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =1-1=0, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =1, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-1=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the coordinates of the second point $C$ are $(0,1,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485525 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124607 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f9ea905-6eb5-55fe-b487-b115406bbc46" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Three-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ce6875-1f98-55e5-bded-8bc92ce46ff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484851} {:answer/id #uuid "386b2c66-1c9c-5668-963d-1846e13ddbee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-2,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484936} {:answer/id #uuid "3954fbd2-b037-5d6f-b09b-6ee284cd02dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484938} {:answer/id #uuid "3d60600e-4e34-5bbc-a23b-24df864c14a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},-1,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484949} {:answer/id #uuid "99ce3a43-46d4-5e6f-9976-ddab31567b2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{9}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d60600e-4e34-5bbc-a23b-24df864c14a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{2},-1,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484949} :answer-field/id #uuid "90d7f323-818d-52fb-8511-4569536d3e65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46ec3a18-4fb6-5ee1-a9e8-e76913b4fb34" :question/math-academy-id "q-131031" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}-x-y$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,-2,3⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}-x-y$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,0,0⟩$ and $b=⟨-1,-2,3⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1,-2,3)-f(0,0,0) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨-1,-2,3⟩-⟨0,0,0⟩) \\\\ 12-0 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,-2,3⟩ \\\\ 12 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,-2,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-1,-1,2z⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-1,-1,2{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨-1,-2,3⟩ \\\\ 12 & =⟨-1,-1,2{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,-2,3⟩ \\\\ 12 & =1+2+6{c}_{3} \\\\ 6{c}_{3} & =9 \\\\ {c}_{3} & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{(-1)} & =\\frac{y-0}{(-2)}=\\frac{z-0}{3} \\\\ -x & =-\\frac{y}{2}=\\frac{z}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=-t,\\,y=-2t,\\,z=3t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=-t,\\,{c}_{2}=-2t,\\,{c}_{3}=3t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation ${c}_{3}=\\frac{3}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3t & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-2(\\frac{1}{2})=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(-\\frac{1}{2},-1,\\frac{3}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485572 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebec626a-be3d-5920-9588-48f3df108944" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem for Two-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7a5f5b-69d3-59c5-9fe5-d1c3092257a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484837} {:answer/id #uuid "1838f568-f154-5028-8a40-70828f862bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484865} {:answer/id #uuid "31c22ae3-b2f9-5039-88b3-e917ad61579f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{8}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484919} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f935b5-6dfe-5bcd-8f1a-c9dd6711bf82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485207} {:answer/id #uuid "d9857530-796f-58fe-9f92-b5bb11123e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{3}{5},\\frac{11}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1838f568-f154-5028-8a40-70828f862bea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484865} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcf7b736-691e-5d1f-a95e-1aa8428199b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6c8dce9-3855-5452-ba32-6512e799f65b" :question/math-academy-id "q-36836" :question/problem "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y)=5{y}^{2}-2x$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,3⟩,$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that the function $f(x,y)=5{y}^{2}-2x$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,2⟩$ and $b=⟨2,3⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,3)-f(0,2) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨2,3⟩-⟨0,2⟩) \\\\ (5(3)^{2}-2(2))-(5(2)^{2}-2(0)) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 21 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨-2,10y⟩, \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨-2,10{c}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21 & =∇f⟨c⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 21 & =⟨-2,10{c}_{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,1⟩ \\\\ 21 & =-4+10{c}_{2} \\\\ 10{c}_{2} & =25 \\\\ {c}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-{y}_{a}}{{y}_{b}-{y}_{a}} & =\\frac{x-{x}_{a}}{{x}_{b}-{x}_{a}} \\\\ \\frac{y-2}{3-2} & =\\frac{x-0}{2-0} \\\\ y-2 & =\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ x & =2y-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =2{c}_{2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking into account that ${c}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2},$ we obtain that ${c}_{1}=2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}-4=1.$ So, the point $C$ that satisfies the mean value theorem is $(1,\\frac{5}{2}).$"}]}
